MOF synthesis always begins with selection of the two main interacting components (primary building units, PBUs): the metallic centres and the bridging organic ligands. It follows that functionality may arise from either (i) the individual PBUs or (ii) the nature and type of connections between them. Because, once formed, coordination polymers are insoluble, their preparation must occur in a single step relying strongly on self-assembly. It is argued that the properties of a given MOF cannot easily be attributed to a specific PBU although, as shown in this review (and supported by many other examples available in the literature), the properties of one of the components often dominate.
Research on MOFs has relied almost entirely on commercially available organic PBUs. Their availability is an advantage for the rapid and inexpensive preparation of MOFs. However, some research groups are now designing and preparing their own organic molecules using (i) non-conventional in situ synthesis and (ii) conventional organic synthesis. In the former, ligand precursors are modified in situ to give PBUs which are subsequently linked to the metal centres. This procedure may involve hydroxylation, decarboxylation, alkylation and hydrolysis, among others.6 In conventional synthesis the organic PBUs are prepared, step-by-step, from organic precursors and the resulting compounds are later employed in the self-assembly of MOFs. An ingenious way of forming organic bridges relies on a posteriori chemical modification of the existing bridges in an already prepared framework. Such post-synthetic modification has motivated much research worldwide. Tanabe and Cohen recently published an excellent review of the subject which we recommend to the reader for further details.7
This review is not an exhaustive literature survey of either the structural features or properties of MOFs. For this the reader is directed to detailed reviews8–22 and book chapters.23,24 Instead, we focus on frameworks prepared from organic PBUs
