## 2 Notation and synthesis details
## 3 Functional materials based on porous frameworks
The ability to support large separations between metal centres, thus creating porosity while maintaining structural robustness, is one of the most desirable properties of novel organic PBUs. However, MOFs exhibiting similar properties or topological features to zeolites or zeo-type compounds are still relatively scarce. According to Sava et al.,25 such materials should: (i) contain large accessible channels without self-penetration; (ii) be charged (typically anionic), with the intra-channel ions being amenable to ion exchange; (iii) be chemically stable in various solvents, particularly water, to allow their application as functional materials.
Although by itself an interesting feature, high porosity can turn MOFs into effective functional materials only when combined with other properties. The strategy behind the design of new organic PBUs by most research groups often relies on long molecules, placing the coordinating groups further apart. In addition, ligand design relies on the formation of more stable chelators, ultimately resulting in robust frameworks. A striking example comes from Lin et al.26 who assumed that increasing the number of functional groups on each side of a ditopic ligand could, on the one hand, promote structural robustness and, on the other, provide an anchor for interactions with guest molecules by way of hydrogen bonding. They designed and prepared $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine- $^{2,2^{\prime},6,6^{\prime}}$ -tetracarboxylic acid $L^{1}$ —Fig. S1 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ) which self-assembles with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ to create a porous framework, $[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(L^{1})]{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , able to endure temperatures as high as $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The evacuated material, $[Z\mathsf{n}_{2}(L^{1})]$ , retains crystallinity with an internal surface area of ca. $313~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ( $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ ). An outstanding crystallographic feature of this structure is that the helical chain of water molecules filling the channels imposes chirality on the material itself.
Symmetrically-substituted organic PBUs are usually preferred because crystal growth can occur in a more uniform and symmetrical fashion. Notable examples based on coordinating carboxylic acid moieties are those of terephthalic and trimesic acids (IUPAC names: 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic and 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acids, respectively). Wong-Foy et al.27 designed and prepared biphenyl- $_{3,4^{\prime}}$ ,5-tricarboxylic acid ( $L^{2}.$ —Fig. S2 in the ESIw), an unsymmetric organic linker still capable of promoting the self-assembly of highly symmetric and porous structures as exemplified by their ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ -based framework: $[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(L^{2})_{2}]{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (Fig. 1a). Guo et al.28 described a highly symmetrical $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ -based rhombohedral porous material: $[\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{3}(L^{2})_{2}]{\cdot}4\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ (Fig. 1b). From these two compounds it can be deduced that the unsymmetric nature of this organic PBU seems to promote simultaneously clustering of metal centres (near the disubstituted ring) and network expansion (via the monosubstituted ring). These MOFs are truly porous and structurally robust enough to endure full channel evacuation as evaluated by BET studies: while UMCM-150 has a surface area of $2300\ \mathrm{~m}^{2}\ \mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ (pore sizes in the 6.5–8.0, $10.5–12.0$ and $12.5{-}13.0\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ranges, consistent with three types of cages), for $[\mathrm{Mg}_{3}(L^{2})_{2}]$ it is about $800~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (average pore size $9.8\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ ).
## 3.1 Ion exchange
One of the most important features of zeolites concerns their ability to act as ion exchangers. As the vast majority of MOFs are electrically neutral, ion exchange in ionic coordination polymers is scarcely explored, although a handful of examples are available in the literature as described briefly in the following paragraphs.
Via an Arbusov-type reaction Plabst and $\mathrm{Bein}^{29}$ designed a novel organic ligand which contains four phosphonic acid moieties in the exo and opposite locations of an aromatic ring: [1,4-phenylenebis(methanetriyl)]tetraphosphonic acid $(L^{3}-$ Fig. S3 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ . Plabst and Bein selected this organic PBU in the hope that it would permit better design and more controllable synthesis of lanthanide-containing MOFs: the large number of coordinating-capable oxygen atoms in the organic PBU fills the requirement of lanthanides for high coordination numbers and hence more crystalline and predictable networks were expected. From a synthetic high-throughput approach three families of compounds were isolated: two composed of neutral layered materials $(\mathrm{[Ln(H}_{5}L^{3})]{\cdot4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ and $[\mathrm{Ln}_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}L^{3})]$ - $8\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , with $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}~=~\mathrm{La}^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{Nd}}^{3+}$ , $\operatorname{Gd}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}$ ) while the third is a porous framework with $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ cations and water molecules filling the channels $\mathrm{(Na[Ln(H_{4}L^{3})]{\cdot}4H_{2}O}$ , with $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}}&{{}=}\end{array}$ $\mathrm{La}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Nd}^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{Gd}}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}$ —see Fig. 2a). The latter material was investigated with respect to its unique ability to exchange $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ for other cations. $^{30}{\mathrm{Na}}^{+}$ could be almost completely exchanged by ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ and ${\mathrm{Rb}}^{+}$ , while ${\mathrm{Cs}}^{+}$ , because of its large ionic radius, was excluded (Fig. 2b). The material excluded divalent cations, which does not occur in zeolites. Dehydration/rehydration studies
