## 3.2 Hydrogen and methane storage
Hydrogen and methane are realistic alternatives to fossil fuels and their storage is of much current interest. The U.S. Department of Energy has published the following storage target values: for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , at near-ambient temperature $-40$ to $85~\mathrm{{^\circC}},$ and amenable pressures ( $<100$ bar), the target is $9.0\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ and $81\mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ by 2015; for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is 180 (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ $(25^{\circ}\mathrm{C},35\mathrm{bar}$ ).34
Following the pioneering work of Kitagawa and collaborators,35,36 many reports appeared on porous MOFs as potential sorbents for hydrogen and methane.
Potential structures for hydrogen or methane sorption may be investigated using in silico methods. Belof et al.37 looked at the adsorption mechanism of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ into an indium framework reported by Liu et al.38 Their conclusions give insightful direction towards the design of the ideal $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ sorbent: (i) alongside a high surface area, the resulting MOFs should also have a large number of relatively small interdigitated pores; (ii) open (low density) frameworks can be detrimental to the adsorption process because only weak $\mathrm{H}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ interactions occur in the middle of the channels; (iii) the inner surface should contain local polar groups to promote $\mathbf{MOF-H}_{2}$ interactions. This last requirement directly concerns the properties of the organic linker. In the structure of Liu et al.38 the spacer contained a connecting ${\mathrm{N}}{=}{\mathrm{N}}$ moiety which allowed significant ${\bf M O F-H}_{2}$ interactions. The organic PBU, azobenzene$_{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetracarboxylate, was prepared according to published procedures.39 A follow-up study on ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ -based MOFs (PCN-10 and $\mathrm{PCN-l1})^{40}$ focuses on the use of very similar double-bondcoupled di-isophthalate organic linkers: azoxybenzene- $^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime},}$ - tetracarboxylic acid ( $L^{7}$ —Fig. S5 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dagger}$ ) and trans-stilbene$^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetracarboxylic acid $L^{8}.$ —Fig. S6 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dagger}_{\mathit{\Phi}_{\mathrm{\varepsilon}}}$ . Azoxybenzene- $^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetracarboxylate (Fig. S7 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dagger})$ was readily transformed into azobenzene- $_{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetracarboxylate $(L^{9})$ by chemical reduction under solvothermal conditions for the preparation of PCN-11. This work complements the theoretical investigations of Belof et al.37 because, for isotypical frameworks, it was possible to observe the differences directly depending on the existence of either a $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}={\mathrm{C}}$ or an ${\bf N}{=}{\bf N}$ bond in the middle section of the organic PBUs which decorate the inner walls of the material. On the one hand, it was found that even though the thermal behaviour of PCN-10 (with a $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}={\mathrm{C}}$ bond; $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(}L^{8})(}\mathrm{H_{2}O)_{2}]}$ 3DMF) and PCN-11 (with a ${\bf N}{=}{\bf N}$ bond; $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(\it L^{9})(H_{2}O)_{2}]\cdot3D M F)}$ is quite similar, the former bond is slightly more capable of permitting permanent porosity after thermal activation: BET surface areas are of 1407 and $1931\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for PCN-10 and -11, respectively.
On the other, the nanosized cages of the structures are capable of effective retention of hydrogen: at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ (760 Torr) PCN-10 and -11 exhibit excess hydrogen uptakes of 2.34 and $2.55~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , respectively. The slightly higher value for the latter material demonstrates the higher affinity of hydrogen towards the ${\bf N}{=}{\bf N}$ bond. It is noted that PCN-11 was found to adsorb large amounts of methane (171 (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 35 bar), approaching the U.S. Department of Energy target.
The theoretical investigations of Belof et al.37 imply that if a ligand promotes large low density frameworks and does not contain polar groups in the inner surface, the uptake of hydrogen should be relatively low. A supporting example was provided by Taylor et al. $^{41}\mathrm{~A~}$ tetrahedral organophosphonate PBU $[L^{10}-1,3,5,7$ -tetrakis(4-phosphonophenyl)adamantane— Fig. S8 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dag]}$ , with a predisposition to form diamondtype networks and whose synthetic procedure was designed and developed by Vasylyev et al.,42 can form a highly porous MOF with ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ : $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(H_{3}}L^{10})(O H)(H_{2}O)_{3}]{\cdot}H_{2}O{\cdot}M e O H.$ Despite the large inner surface area $\cdot182\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption), the compound failed to retain hydrogen with uptake of only $0.24~\mathrm{wt\%}$ .
A mixed approach of ligand design/selection (from commercial sources) towards the rational preparation of functional materials comes from Yaghi’s laboratory. Based on the stable open framework MOF- $\cdot5^{43}$ they reported an isoreticular extension when using organic linkers which could expand the pores while retaining the cubic topology of the parent material.44 Members of this series were referred to as IRMOFs. 4,5,9,10-Tetrahydropyrene2,7-dicarboxylic acid ( $L^{11}$ —Fig. S9 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ) is based on a rigid canvas composed of four fused rings and it was prepared using the published procedure of Connor et al. which permits the isolation of large amounts of specific pyrene derivatives.45 IRMOF-12, $[\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}(L^{11})_{3}]{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , is one of the most porous materials of the series. Nevertheless, it retains structural integrity when evacuated and it gives type I adsorption isotherms when exposed to a number of solvents. These features are similar to those of IRMOF-6, which is an excellent methane adsorbent capable of storing at 36 atm nearly $70\%$ of the methane contained in a gas cylinder at 205 atm. Frost et al. considered the potential applications of all series of the IRMOF materials as hydrogen sorbents and showed that they can meet the aforementioned requirements of the U.S. Department of Energy.46
Wu et al. showed that zeolitic imidazolate-based frameworks (ZIFs) can capture $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ through interactions with the organic backbone and suggested that modification of the linkers, rather than metal types in ZIFs, may be important to optimize these materials for higher storage capacity.47 Following this insight, Choi et al.48 designed a new highly symmetric tetragonal framework based on ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ centres and 1,4-di( $1H$ -pyrazol-4-yl)benzene ( $L^{12}$ —Fig. S10 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dag},$ : $\mathrm{[Co(}L^{12})]{\cdot}2\mathrm{D}\mathrm{MF}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . This organic PBU was prepared following the procedure developed by Lozan et al.49 For this material, at ca. 30 bar and $77\mathrm{K}$ the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake is about $3.1~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , reaching $5.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $50~\mathrm{K}$ (ca. 10 bar).
