2,4,6-tris[4- $2H$ -tetrazol-5-yl)phenyl]1,3,5-triazine ( $L^{14}.$ —Fig. S11 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dagger}$ ). It was suggested that the close proximity between hydrogen atoms in the core of $L^{13}$ would promote a distorted molecule which, in turn, could prevent effective $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interactions between neighbouring ligands in the final structures (Fig. 3—top left). Conversely, $L^{14}$ is almost planar due to the hydrogen-free triazine canvas in the core of the molecule. Solvothermal reactions (in DMF at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ with these two organic PBUs lead to crystals with almost identical unit cell parameters: $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}[(\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{Cl})_{3}(L^{13})_{8}]_{2}\cdot11\mathrm{Cu}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\cdot8\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\cdot$ 120DMF, $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}[(\mathrm{Mn}_{4}\mathrm{Cl})_{3}(L^{14})_{8}]_{2}\cdot25\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\cdot15\mathrm{MeOH}\cdot95\mathrm{DMF}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}[(\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{Cl})_{3}(L^{14})_{8}]_{2}{\cdot}x(\mathrm{solvent})$ . When $L^{14}$ was used, the bodycentred unit cell was two-fold interpenetrated by identical frameworks (Fig. 3—top right). In short, the twisted nature of $L^{13}$ prevented interpenetration. This experimental study reflects the broad theoretical conclusions of Belof et al., in particular their assessment of the optimal size and type of channels:37 while the non-interpenetrated (with $L^{13}$ ) and doubleinterpenetrated (with $L^{14}$ ) materials exhibit almost identical $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptakes at ambient temperature $(0.5\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 68 bar), at $77\mathrm{K}$ the catenated framework has a much higher uptake $(4.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 80 bar) than the highly porous one $2.8~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 30 bar)—Fig. 3, bottom.
Third generation MOF architectures, characterized by large polyhedral cavities, can also be designed to include functional groups capable of sequestering hydrogen.51 The replacement of the 5th position of 1,3-benzenedicarboxylic acid by a tetrazolyl group (5-( $1H.$ -tetrazol-5-yl)isophthalic acid— $L^{15}-$ Fig. S12 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dag})^{52,53}$ permitted the design and synthesis of a large ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ -based framework, $\mathrm{[Cu_{6}O(\it L^{15})_{3}(H_{2}O)_{9}(N O_{3})]}$  $15\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , shown to have a high affinity for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ with uptake at $77~\mathrm{K}$ being $2.4~\mathrm{wt\%}$ . More recently, the research groups of Snurr and ${\mathrm{Hupp}}^{54}$ used computational design to predict the adsorption properties of a novel highly porous third-generation material. Using as the starting premise a previously published cubic structure featuring copper paddlewheel clusters, they designed a novel expanded hexacarboxylic acid ligand able to retain the structural topology of the parent framework while promoting the formation of larger structural cavities: 1,3,5-tris[(1,3-dicarboxylic acid-5-(4-(ethynyl)phenyl))ethynyl]benzene 1 $L^{16}$ —Fig. S13 in the ESIw). Despite the considerable separation between the metallic clusters due to the presence of the new mesopores, $\mathrm{[Cu}_{3}(L^{16})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}]$ does not exhibit interpenetration. As expected, the framework exhibits a remarkable $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake of about $164\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (at 70 bar and $77\mathrm{K}$ ), which is well within the limits of the U.S. Department of Energy.
## 3.3 Sequestration of carbon dioxide
MOF-based materials can play a decisive role in technological and environmental applications when employed for the separation of environmentally undesirable chemicals. $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is one of the main gases in industrial emissions and current capture strategies (such as chemisorption and amine reaction in a fluidized bed) are ineffective and expensive to maintain. For industrial viability, a MOF should combine several features: (i) porosity with accessible channels; (ii) thermal stability; (iii) low production cost and the (iv) ability of the inner surface to interact with guests (or to be activated to do so) in order to potentiate adsorption. A handful of MOFs44,55 and $Z\mathrm{IFs}^{56}$ are able to meet these requirements while effectively capturing and separating $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from CO, $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ or ${\bf N}_{2}$ (see, for example, the account from Yaghi’s group describing the performance of several ZIF materials). For a broad discussion of MOF-based materials in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation we refer the reader to the excellent critical review by Sholl and collaborators.57
Heterocyclic ligands containing nitrogen donor atoms, in particular imidazoles, have been demonstrated to be highly selective for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .58 With this in mind, Pachfule et al.59 designed and prepared a novel ligand containing both tetrazole and pyrimidine groups: 2-( $1H.$ -tetrazol-5-yl)pyrimidine $L^{17}.$ —Fig. S14 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ). This organic PBU forms an interesting twodimensional network with ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{[Cu(\itL^{17})_{1.5}(O H)_{0.5}]}$ (Cu-TP-1), having (6,3) topology and hexagonal pores ca. 6 A˚ in diameter (Fig. 4a). Layers pack in a parallel fashion, leading to a supramolecular porous structure with a type I sorption isotherm and, as expected, with a considerable number of uncoordinated nitrogen atoms lining the inner walls. As a consequence, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake (Fig. 4b) is comparable to that of ZIF-95,56 known to perform better in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption than the commercial solution BPL carbon.
The ${\mathrm{La}}^{3+}$ -based MOF structures prepared by Lama et al.,60 $\mathrm{[La}(L^{18})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{\small{[La(}}L^{18})\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O)}_{2}\mathrm{]}{\cdot}2\mathrm{D}\mathrm{MF}$ , contain oxygen atoms lining the inner walls instead of nitrogen. These atoms do not belong to the coordinating groups but to the organic backbone itself: 5-(4-carboxyphenoxy)isophthalic acid 1 $L^{18}.$ —Fig. S15 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ).61 Frameworks exhibit singlecrystal-to-single-crystal solvent exchange at room temperature (e.g., water in the parent framework could be displaced by pyridine, ethanol or acetone) and are stable up to $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ At $0~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ evacuated materials show a remarkable selectivity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ but not for ${\bf N}_{2}$ . Clearly, further studies on this particular system are needed, including the exploration of the presence of $\mathrm{La}^{3+}$ . This is an indication that the framework may accommodate other optically active lanthanide cations such as $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ . In this context, we envisage that measuring the photoluminescent properties of the material may eventually provide insights into the sorption mechanism of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .
## 3.4 Gas separation
The simplest way to separate molecules is based on their size (‘‘size-exclusion effect’’) and many types of materials, such as zeolites, nanotubes, ionic liquids, MOFs and ZIFs have been studied for this purpose. Gases are particularly difficult to separate, especially when their molecular radii differ only slightly.62 Thus, frameworks need to possess specific functional properties, or be amenable to chemical modification, to facilitate the interaction of a given gas with its inner surface. The potentiality of a given MOF may be addressed theoretically,63 but practical framework design is not trivial.
Miller et al.64 prepared $N,N^{\prime}$ -piperazinebis(methylenephosphonic acid) ( $L^{4}.$ —Fig. S4 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ), via a modified Mannich reaction.65 This molecule was designed to be (i) highly flexible, (ii) to allow various possible coordination sites and coordinating modes, and (iii) to possess nitrogen atoms far from the metallic coordination sphere so as to interact with guest species. The isolated highly symmetrical framework, $[\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(L^{4})]{\cdot}x\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with $x=8,2$ or 0 (coined as Ni-STA-12), has elliptical channels $(8\times9\mathrm{\AA})$ and permanent porosity after full evacuation of the housed water molecules (type I microporous material—Fig. 5a). Adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , CO, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ was monitored through infrared spectroscopy to ascertain the sites responsible for the capture of these molecules. Between $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , the evacuated material is a better absorber than the as-synthesized one by a factor of ca. 10 (at 1 bar)—Fig. 5b and c. This considerable difference occurs because of the strong interactions of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with all coordinative unsaturated $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ sites. A similar conclusion was reached for the $_\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}$ system: the material can readjust its structure so that the CO molecules can interact better with the Lewis $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ sites, but the process suffers from slow kinetics. Concerning $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , no evidence for interactions with the uncoordinated $\scriptstyle\mathbf{P=}\mathbf{0}$ groups was found. Some interactions with the metal centres were registered but, as for CO, diffusion accessibility was limited by the surrounding coordinating groups. Prior to this report, the same research group published a handful of purely structural studies on materials with $L^{4}$ : MIL-91 frameworks with titanium and aluminium;66 a detailed investigation on the reaction conditions which promote distinct frameworks with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ;67 three novel ${\mathrm{Y}}^{3+}$ -, $\operatorname{Gd}^{3+}.$ -, and ${\mathrm{Nd}}^{3+}$ -based frameworks.68
Groves et al. reported earlier69 the preparation of a ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ - based framework assembled from the racemic 2-methylated derivative 2-methyl- $N,N^{\prime}$ -piperazinebis(methylenephosphonic acid) ( $L^{5}$ —Fig. S4 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ : $\mathrm{(NH_{4})}[\mathrm{Co_{8}}(L^{5})_{6}(\mathrm{CH_{3}C O_{2}})_{3}.$ - $\mathrm{(OH,H_{2}O)_{6}l\cdot1l H_{2}O}.$ . In this framework the organic PBU is highly disordered with the methyl groups projecting into the large channels. Despite the apparent crystallographic porosity, the material could not be fully evacuated. This feature was attributed to the presence of charged species in the channels: the material could only be isolated in the presence of acetate in the reaction medium, which most likely is housed within the channels, thus trapping the solvent molecules via strong hydrogen bonds. A recent structural report from the same research group showed that when racemic $L^{5}$ was self-assembled (under hydrothermal conditions) with ${\mathrm{Y}}^{3+}$ or ${\mathrm{Gd}}^{3+}$ two novel frameworks were obtained: $[\mathrm{Y}_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}L^{5})_{3}]{\cdot}5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $[\mathrm{Gd}_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}L^{5})_{3}].$ - $3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . The use of enantiomerically-pure $R\cdot$ - or $S{-}L^{5}$ promoted instead the formation of a different porous framework, $[\mathrm{Y}_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}L^{5})_{3}]{\cdot}7\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (coined STA-13).70
The organic PBU lithium $7,7^{\prime},8,8^{\prime}$ -tetracyanoquinodimethanide(1-) ( $L^{19}.$ —Fig. S16 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ) employed by Kitagawa and collaborators62,71 can be either straightforwardly prepared from the commercially available $7,7^{\prime},8,8^{\prime}$ -tetracyano$p$ -quinodimethane (also known as TCNQ), or from condensation of malononitrile and 1,4-ciclohexanedione.73 This ligand was used to prepare $[\mathrm{Zn}(L^{19})(4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{-bpy)}]{\cdot}6\mathrm{MeOH}$ , a MOF which despite not being able to be fully evacuated exhibits singlecrystal-to-single-crystal transformation, with colour change, when MeOH molecules are exchanged by toluene, ethylbenzene, anisole, benzonitrile, and nitrobenzene.74 It was later reported that the molecule dimerizes in situ (solvothermal reaction) leading to the formation of 1,2-bis[4-(dicyanomethyl)phenyl]ethane1,1,2,2-tetracarbonitrile ( $L^{20}$ —Fig. S17 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dagger} $ which acts as an effective intermetallic bridge in $[Z\mathrm{n}(L^{20})(4,4^{\prime}{-}\mathrm{br}$ y)].62 The inner walls of the MOF are, in this way, highly rich in electrons. This feature led the authors to investigate the framework behaviour towards the adsorption of a number of gases. Gate-type sorption (i.e., no uptake at low concentration followed by a dramatic increase after a given threshold) was observed for $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and NO. This was not observed for other gases such as CO, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , Ar, ${\bf N}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ .
## 3.5 Molecular sieving
Ligand design can control both the apertures of channels (for sieving based on the ‘‘size-exclusion effect’’) or distinct binding guest affinities by, for example, including specific sites capable of intermolecular recognition. A striking example of the latter strategy concerns the $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ -based porous network reported by Hasegawa et al. (see further details in Section 5). $^{75}\mathrm{\bfA}$ ligand design principle for controllable framework pore sizes is that of Eddaoudi et al.44 concerning the discovery of the IRMOF series (see Section 3.2). Overall, the number of a priori designed materials in the literature that fall into this category is small and sieving is usually found by serendipity. Nevertheless, several known MOF structures have been developed into functional membranes capable of sieving.21,76
Yue et al.77 employed a chiral ligand [(S)- $N_{\l}$ -(phosphonomethyl)proline— $\cdot L^{21}$ —Fig. S18 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dag]}$ obtained from the reaction of (S)-proline with phosphoric acid. This organic PBU was reacted with lanthanide centres to form helical chiral chains strongly hydrogen-bonded to each other, leading to a complex supramolecular architecture (Fig. 6a): $[\mathrm{Ln}(\mathrm{H}_{2}L^{21})_{3}]$  $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (with $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}~=~\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{Gd}}^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{Tb}}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}$ ). The product shows permanent porosity (type I isotherm) as evaluated from ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption measurements for $[\mathrm{Dy(H}_{2}L^{21})_{3}]$ - $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . The same behaviour was observed for water and methanol, but at ambient temperature the material rejected larger molecules such as ethanol because of constraints associated with the channel aperture (Fig. 6b and c).
An interesting example of sieving associated with molecular recognition comes from Yuan et al.78 The unsymmetrical Schiff base ligand $3{-}[(E)-\{[(1R,2R)-2$ -aminocyclohexyl]imino}- methyl]-4-hydroxybenzoic acid ( $L^{22}$ —Fig. S19 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dagger}$ ), prepared via a Reimer–Tiemann reaction, is not the usual organic PBU as it contains three distinct types of coordinating groups, structurally located in positions which typically do not promote polymerization. The molecule itself is highly flexible. Nevertheless, the authors envisaged that the resulting materials might present some level of porosity with the channels being internally lined by aliphatic moieties and nitrogen atoms. The material resulting from hydrothermal reaction at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was a homochiral polymeric chain which, by way of strong supramolecular interactions, self-organized into a porous lamellar structure: $\mathrm{[Cu}_{2}(L^{22})_{2}]{\cdot}2\mathrm{MeOH}$ . The lower dimensionality (2D instead of 3D) of the polymer is not detrimental but confers some structural flexibility when interacting with guest solvents, leading to their effective discrimination: C1 to C5 alcohols could be easily included in the crystal structures by tuning the helical pitches and the interlayer spaces; the longer C6 and C7 molecules were, however, excluded. This network can also be employed for chiral resolution (i.e., the $R$ -material can preferentially adsorb $R$ alcohols). Moreover, this organic ligand has a relatively straightforward large scale synthesis at low cost. These are two highly attractive features for the potential industrial use of the polymeric material.
## 4 Photoluminescent materials
The crystal structure of MOFs provides a versatile playground for engineering optically-active compounds. The organic and inorganic components can be simultaneously ‘‘tuned’’ both by chemically rendering the organic bridges functional or by changing the metallic centre(s) (or designing a mixture of, typically, lanthanides) so as to modify the optical properties of the final product. Light emission can therefore arise either from the individual organic or inorganic components, or from compounds resulting from their interconnection. In addition, because MOFs are crystalline, intermetallic distances, interaction angles, etc., are fully described a priori, which might be of particular relevance for improving the observed properties. Another great advantage of MOFs is that metallic optical centres can be easily sensitized by appropriate selection of the organic ligand (known as the antenna effect). For additional technical details, the reader is referred to a number of reviews on the general aspects of engineering optical centres into materials, particularly those by Bu¨ nzli and Piguet,79 Allendorf et al.80 and Rocha et al.81
## 4.1 Lanthanide-based photoluminescence
Many reports of MOFs exhibiting metal-based photoluminescence (PL) are available. Typically, authors change lanthanide optical centres (e.g., $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{Tb}}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Er}^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}$ ) in a systematic fashion, thus allowing fine-tuning of the PL between the visible (red, green) and the infrared region of the spectrum. This strategy will not be discussed in this review. The focus instead is on the design of specific organic PBUs which may or may not sensitize selected lanthanide centres, or permit the effective coordination to lanthanides. Once the experimental conditions needed to produce a network are known, the modification of the lanthanide metallic centre is usually relatively straightforward (except in those cases where the lanthanide contraction effect dominates the self-assembly and constrains a certain type or family of materials).
Reddy’s group82 prepared a series of 4-benzyloxybenzoic acid derivatives ( $L^{23}$ —Fig. S20 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ) to investigate the influence of the substituents in the PL. Organic modification was performed by including electron releasing (OMe) and electron withdrawing $(\mathsf{N O}_{2})$ substituents to produce two novel ligands: 4-benzyloxy-3-methoxybenzoic acid ( $L^{24}$ —Fig. S20 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ) and 4-benzyloxy-3-nitrobenzoic acid $L^{25}.$ —Fig. S20 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ). Structures based on the $-\mathrm{H}$ $\mathrm{(^{0}[L n_{2}(}L^{23})_{6}.$ $\mathrm{(DMSO)}_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}])$ and –OMe $\mathrm{\Lambda^{(1}}[\mathrm{Ln}_{2}(L^{24})_{6}(\mathrm{MeOH})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}]$ where $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}=\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{Gd}}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Tb}}^{3+}$ ) derivatives were characterized by crystallography and shown to be formed by dimeric metallic units. The latter material is formed by a one-dimensional coordination polymer. It was demonstrated (mainly based on the overall quantum yields, $\Phi$ ) that the photosensitization of the lanthanide centres was strongly affected by the type of substituents (always compared to the $L^{23}$ -based structures): while the electron releasing group improves the emission $[\Phi(\mathrm{in}\%)$ : Eu- $L^{24}$ $0.49\pm\:0.05$ ; $\mathrm{Tb}{-}L^{24}\ 33\ \pm\ 3]$ , the electron withdrawing $-\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ moiety quenches it [F (in%): Eu-L25 0.04  0.01; Tb-L25 $0.10\pm0.01]$ by way of an energy dissipation channel. The lanthanide lifetimes, $\tau$ (in ms), remained more or less constant for all members of the series: $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+5}\mathrm{D}_{0}380\pm20$ to $420\pm30~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}~^{5}\mathrm{D}_{4}~1040\pm50$ to $1240\pm10~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ . Even though only the structure with $L^{24}$ is polymeric (and well within the scope of this review), this work is a remarkable illustration of how substituents can have a great influence on optical properties. These conclusions are supported experimentally by the measurement of physical parameters (e.g., radiative and non-radiate decay rates, overall quantum yields, line transition lifetimes), all in line with the concepts suggested by Rocha et al.81
Under aqueous media, and for a typical monodentate coordination, lanthanide centres preferentially select ‘‘hard’’ oxygen atoms to ‘‘soft’’ nitrogen donors. The use of pyrazolate-, triazolate- or tetrazolate-based PBUs is thus detrimental in the self-assembly of MOFs. For example, a search in the Cambridge Structural Database (Version 5.31 with four updates) for lanthanide-based polymeric structures with tetrazolates yielded a single family of isotypical one-dimensional chains, reported by Lin et al., based on a commercial tetrazole derivative and formate anions.83 Thus, the use of tetrazole derivatives requires modification of the linkers to render them more prone to coordination to the lanthanides. Li et al.84 used the synthetic route developed by Einberg85 in the early 1970s to modify 5-aminotetrazole to (5-amino- $1H.$ -tetrazol-1-yl)acetic acid $(L^{26}-$ Fig. S21 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ). This molecule was used to construct a number of photoluminescent one-dimensional $\mathrm{([Ln(}L^{26})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}]\mathrm{Cl}$ with $\begin{array}{r c l}{\displaystyle\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}}&{=}&{\mathrm{Gd}^{3+}}\end{array}$ and $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ ; $\mathrm{[Tb}(L^{26})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}]\mathrm{Cl}$ and lamellar structures $([\mathrm{Ln}(L^{26})_{2}(\mathrm{MeOH})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\mathrm{Cl}]$ with $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Ln}}^{3+}\ =$ $\mathrm{Pr}^{3+}$ , ${\bf N}\mathsf{d}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ ). Qu et al.86 recently prepared 5-( $1H\cdot$ tetrazol-5-yl)isophthalic acid ( $L^{27}.$ —Fig. S22 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dag},$ , using a different methodology from that of Eddaoudi’s group.51 The organic PBU was used to produce a lamellar material with ${\mathrm{Gd}}^{3+}$ formulated as $\mathrm{[Gd}(L^{27})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}]{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . We note that for $L^{27}$ the lower preference of lanthanides for nitrogen leads only to a two-dimensional material while when $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ is used a structure with large polyhedral cages results (Section 3.2). In these two lanthanide-based materials the coordination spheres are mainly composed of oxygen atoms from the carboxylate moieties. However, it is the tetrazolate units that are responsible for the polymerization process. The PL properties of these families of compounds were not explored in detail. Likewise, Yue et al.77 did not investigate the photoluminescent properties of their system with (S)-N(phosphonomethyl)proline (see the previous subsection). We believe it is important to revisit these systems so as to evaluate the effect of the inclusion of heterocyclic rings with a large number of nitrogen atoms on the photoluminescence of the MOFs.
The PL of lanthanide cations is severely quenched through non-radiative processes involving thermal deactivation from O–H, N–H and C–H groups within the organic PBUs. These detrimental oscillators can be replaced by, for example, $\mathrm{C-F}$ or $\mathrm{C-D}$ through organic synthesis. Deuteration is not, however, a permanent solution as the compound can revert to its normal form when in contact with air or water. The use of $\mathrm{C-F}$ groups is more desirable and a handful of reports of interesting lanthanide-based polymeric structures is already available.87,88 For example, Chen et al.89 employed commercial 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid $\textstyle(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdc})$ and its fluorinated form (2,3,5,6-tetrafluoro-1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid- ${\mathrm{.H}}_{2}{\mathrm{bdc}}{\mathrm{-F}}_{4})$ to produce $\mathrm{[Er_{2}(b d c)_{3}(D M F)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}]\cdot H_{2}O}$ and $\mathrm{[Er_{2}(b d c–F_{4})_{3}(D M F)(H_{2}O)]}$ DMF. They observed a considerable increase in the PL intensity, but did not measure absolute physical quantities (such as quantum yield or lifetimes). Similar strategies to improve the PL properties can be found throughout the literature, with a considerable number of commercially-available linkers being able to satisfy the requirements of the researchers. Within the scope of this review we could not, however, find a single ligand for the self-assembly of MOFs specifically designed to have the smallest possible number of quenchers.
## 4.2 Ligand-based fluorescence
An emerging class of photoluminescent MOFs is based on networks containing optically active and strongly fluorescent organic linkers with long lifetimes. For example, the research group of Allendorf has reported a family of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ -based scintillating MOFs based on commercial trans- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -stilbene dicarboxylic acid which respond to ionizing radiation by emitting light in the visible region of the spectrum.90,91 The combination of such interesting PL features with permanent framework porosity opens the possibility of preparing a new class of guest-responsive functional MOFs: ligand-centred emission is in most cases strongly dependent on the presence (or absence) of guest species, their chemical nature and of their interaction with the organic PBUs of the MOF architectures.91,92
A remarkable example of a functional MOF belonging to this family of materials was developed by the research groups of Furukawa and Kitagawa.93 They envisaged that their known 3D cubic, two-fold interpenetrated framework with general formula $[\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (dicarboxylate)2(amine)] could also be prepared by using as an organic linker a dipyridyl derivative of the photoactive 1,4,5,8-naphthalenediimide molecule: $N,N^{\prime}$ di-(4-pyridyl)-1,4,5,8-naphthalenetetracarboxydiimide $(L^{28}-$ Fig. S23 in the ESIw). Even though this newly-designed linker predictably has low fluorescent quantum yield, the authors deduced that its organic core could interact strongly with simple aromatic volatile organic compounds (VOC). The prepared materials exhibited a strong colour change in the visible region of the spectrum according to the VOC adsorbed: the straightforward incorporation of molecules such as benzene, toluene, xylene, anisole and iodobenzene into the nanopores of the evacuated interpenetrated $[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(L^{28})]$ framework, led to new adducts (i.e., $[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(L^{28})]\mathrm{>VOC)}$ displaying intense blue, cyan, green, yellow and red photoluminescence, respectively (Fig. 7). The authors have further demonstrated that this chemoresponse was of a non-linear nature due to the coupling of structural transformation with the amount of incorporated guest molecules. A few years before, Suh and collaborators94 prepared the porous two-fold interpenetrated $[Z\mathrm{n}_{4}\mathrm{O}(L^{29})_{2}]$ 3DMFEtOH material, based on the tripodal $4{,}4{^\prime}{,}4{^\prime}{}^{\prime}$ -nitrilotrisbenzoic acid ligand $(L^{29}-$ Fig. S24 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ) originally reported by Dapperheld et al.,95 with the observed luminescence being strongly dependent on the nature of the guest molecules: the immersion of the evacuated framework in pyridine, methanol or benzene produced a marked modification of the maximum of wavelength emission upon excitation at $340\ \mathrm{nm}$ (435, 456 and $466~\mathrm{nm}$ respectively).
Stylianou et al.96 engineered a new tetracarboxylate ligand designed around the strongly fluorescent pyrene core known by its high lifetime and quantum yield ( $113\mathrm{~ms~}$ and 0.65, respectively, measured in ethanol at $293\mathrm{~K~}$ : $4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime},4^{\prime\prime\prime}.$ - (pyrene-1,3,6,8-tetrayl)tetrabenzoic acid ( ${\bf\nabla}L^{30}$ —Fig. S25 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dag},$ . On the one hand, the presence of the four exolocated carboxylic acid groups in $L^{30}$ indicated the formation of open framework-type compounds. On the other hand, the use of $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ as the metallic centre, a cation with a fully occupied valence shell $(d^{10})$ , limited considerably the existence of non-radiative emission channels. The prepared noninterpenetrated $[\mathrm{In}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(L^{30})]$ (guests) framework (solvents: DMF, dioxane and water) was shown to exhibit permanent porosity upon evacuation, even though overall crystallinity was significantly reduced. The framework could be fully regenerated by exposure to some solvents (e.g., DMF), while to others the process was only partially completed. The authors further demonstrated that the fluorescent properties of the framework were strongly dependent on the type and amount of incorporated guest molecules: $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ induces the largest shift (when compared to the as-prepared material) with the observed lifetime being reduced to approximately half of the original value (to ca. $0.054~\mathrm{{ms}}^{\cdot}$ ); dioxane does not produce a significant shift but quenches significantly the emission (being only ca. $36\%$ of the original material); exposure of the evacuated material to DMF restores the original emission features; toluene produces a similar effect to that registered for dioxane. The authors concluded that the observed features could be explained by different interactions of the guests with the organic bridges of the framework, in particular with the pyrene core.
## 5 Heterogeneous catalysts
Alongside the quest for porosity, application of MOFs as heterogeneous catalysts has also been actively investigated from the very early days of the field. For a broad perspective of the research performed to date the reader is urged to consult the excellent review by Corma and collaborators.8 The objective of this intensive research on catalytic-active MOFs was not to replace zeolites as the most commonly used industrial heterogenous catalysts, but to fill a number of important gaps never addressed by zeo-type compounds: for instance, in enantioselective heterogeneous catalysis. To date, the production of chiral chemicals and fine chemicals relies almost exclusively on the use of homogeneous catalysts, which is subject to a number of limitations (e.g., separation, re-utilisation, loss of activity, among others). The design of specific organic PBUs for the construction of active MOFs may provide a solution. An elegant attempt to address this need was reported by Seo et al.,33 who designed a chiral organic PBU from D-tartaric acid $[(4R,5R)$ -2,2-dimethyl-5-[(pyridin-4-ylamino)carbonyl]-1,3- dioxolane-4-carboxylic acid— $\cdot L^{31}$ —Fig. S26 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dag]}$ . This PBU was self-assembled with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ cations into a homochiral porous framework $(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O})_{2}[\mathrm{}\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\upmu_{3}\mathrm{-O})(\mathrm{H}L^{31})_{6}]\cdot12\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , known as D-POST-1. The enantiomorphic L-structure could be obtained in a similar way by using the corresponding enantiomer: [(4S,5S)-2,2-dimethyl-5-[(4-pyridinylamino)carbonyl]-1,3- dioxolane-4-carboxylic acid]. Firstly, 2,4-dinitrophenyl acetate could readily undergo transesterification with ethanol at ambient temperature with ethyl acetate being produced in $77\%$ yield after $55\mathrm{~h~}$ . The process is size-dependent since for larger alcohols (e.g., isobutanol, neopentanol and 3,3,3-triphenyl-1-propanol) the rate of reaction was considerably smaller or negligible. This indicates that the catalytic conversion clearly occurs within the channels. Further evidence comes from the fact that the chemically modified (via $N$ -alkylation) POST-1 material was catalytically inert. Secondly, racemic 1-phenyl-2- propanol could be thermally converted into the corresponding esters with approximately $8\%$ of enantiomeric excess (in favour of the $S$ or $R$ enantiomer, respectively) in the presence of D- or L-POST-1, respectively. The same research group later reported promising results for the photoisomerisations of $(Z)$ - cyclooctene into the optically-active $(S)–(+)–(E)$ -cyclooctene in ca. $5.4\%$ of enantiomeric excess.97
Another work focuses on chiral 2,2-diethoxy-1,1-binaphthalene-6,6-diphosphonic acid $\cdot L^{32}$ —Fig. S27 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ).98 Since the discovery of the potentialities of BINAP by Noyori and Ohkuma99 most asymmetric homogeneous catalytic systems have been developed around its $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -substituted binaphthalene organic canvas. Such molecules have, however, a very limited ability to promote polymerisation because the coordinating groups are pointing towards endo positions. Evans et al.98 realized that if coordinating-capable phosphonate groups were in the $^{6,6^{\prime}}$ positions, the formation of higher dimensional networks could then be achieved. This resulted in the isolation of homochiral porous lanthanide-based lamellar materials, structurally robust to solvent evacuation (at ambient temperature): $[\mathrm{Ln}(\mathrm{H}_{2}L^{32})(\mathrm{H}_{3}L^{32})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}]{\cdot}x\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (with $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\ =\ \mathrm{La}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Pr}^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{Nd}}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{Gd}}^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{Tb}}^{3+}$ and $x~=~9–14)$ . Because of the presence of both Lewis and Brønsted acid sites, these materials could be used as active heterogeneous catalysts: in ring opening of meso-carboxylic anhydrides (1-naphthaldehyde and propionaldehyde were converted to their cyanohydrin products with $>50\%$ yield) and cyanosilylation of aldehydes (conversion of benzaldehyde into mandelonitrile with yield of about $70\%$ ). In opposition to that registered for POST-1, the equal catalytic activity regardless of the size of the aldehyde shows that the lamellar structure can easily accommodate substrates of different sizes. Another interesting application of these chiral materials reported by Evans et al.,98 which falls into the area of molecular sieving (Section 3.5), concerns their potential use for enantiomeric separation. This was demonstrated from preliminary tests using racemic trans-1,2-diaminocyclohexane. A year later the same research group expanded the use of this ligand to the first row of $d_{}$ -block elements, reporting a series of compounds with $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ .100
We have already demonstrated that porosity cannot by itself induce significant functionalities in MOFs besides molecular sieving. It may, however, if the channels are wide and accessible enough to allow post-synthesis functionalization of the inner walls as demonstrated for POST-1.33 Nevertheless, due to the combined high structural flexibility of MOFs and their low thermal and chemical robustness, this strategy only recently became more common. To engineer a truly functional porous MOF, which could selectively stabilize the guest/ intermediate species of a catalytic reaction, Kitagawa and collaborators designed a novel organic ligand, $\mathbf{N}^{1},\mathbf{N}^{3},\mathbf{N}^{5}.$ - tri(pyridin-4-yl)benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxamide ( ${\boldsymbol{L}}^{33}$ —Fig. S28 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ), which contains amide groups capable of hydrogen bonding with the guest species (Fig. 8a).75 The prepared $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ - based porous network, $[\mathrm{Cd}(L^{33})_{2}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}]{\cdot6}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}{\cdot2}\mathrm{D}\mathrm{MF}$ , heterogeneously catalyses the Knoevenagel condensation between benzaldehyde and malononitrile ( $98\%$ conversion—Fig. 8b). It is further highly selective toward this substrate because other molecules showed negligible conversions (Fig. 8b, table). The combined effect of the hydrogen-bonding recognition with the ‘‘size-exclusion phenomenon’’ from the accessible channels of the MOF justifies the high selectivity observed for this material. The desolvated material (obtained, for example, under vacuum at $140~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for ca. 8 hours) is catalytically inert because of the collapse of the accessible channels, which further supports the assumption of the occurrence of the catalytic activity inside the MOF crystals.
A truly multifunctional system was recently discovered by the research groups of Atwood and Thallapally by using the tetrahedral organic PBU tetrakis[4-(carboxyphenyl)oxymethyl]- methane ${\mathrm{~L}}^{34}.$ —Fig. S29 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dagger}$ ).101 They isolated three distinct isomeric frameworks: $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{O}(L^{34})(\mathrm{DMF})]{\cdot}3\mathrm{DMF}$ , $[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(L^{34})(\mathrm{DMF})]$ DMF and $[Z\mathrm{n}(L^{34})]$ (Fig. 9a). The two former materials were highly active in Friedel–Crafts alkylations (Fig. 9b):102 whereas for toluene there was a high selectivity towards the para orientated material $(>99\%$ of conversion); for biphenyl the selectivity was even higher with conversion reaching $100\%$ . These remarkable differences (when compared with $[Z\mathrm{n}(L^{34})])$ were explained as due to the formation of free metal sites after the removal of the DMF molecules. The products are also effective in gas separation (Fig. 9c and d) as they preferentially absorb $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over ${\bf N}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at ambient temperature.103 The same behaviour was described in more recent studies: the three-fold interpenetrated $\mathrm{[Cd}_{6}(L^{34})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}]$  guest framework104 has a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake estimated at $6.0\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ (i.e., $30\mathrm{mL}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at STP), found to be six times higher than that of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ ; the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ -based framework with PtS topology, $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(}L^{34})(H_{2}O)_{2}]$ , has a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake of $24\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 35 bar.105
## 6 Magnetic materials
While organic linkers are typically diamagnetic, the magnetic properties of a MOF depend on the magnetic coupling between the metallic nodes (i.e., the inorganic PBU) which presents a great challenge to crystal engineers. The superexchange mechanism involving the orbitals of the magnetic centres depends strongly on their relative orientation and their spatial separation. The promotion of low-density open framework MOF structures by increasing the length of the spacer is thus detrimental to the final magnetic properties, leading in the vast majority of cases to a hybrid network with isolated magnetic centres.
To address the interplay between porosity and magnetic exchange, Maspoch et al.106 designed novel organic ligands based on persistent polyfunctionalised radicals, which are subvalent molecules (i.e., with one fewer bond than expected). A myriad of distinct stable radicals has been described.107 Veciana transposed these molecules into supramolecular chemistry by using polychlorinated triphenylmethyl radicals (the chlorine atoms help to stabilise the central carbon), derivatized with three carboxylic acid groups located in exo positions. Tris(2,3,5,6-tetrachloro-4-carboxy)methyl radical 1 $L^{35}.$ —Fig. S30 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ) was self-assembled with $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ to produce the MOROF-1 magnetic network, isolated from slow diffusion methods and composed of large crystals: $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(L^{35})_{2}(\mathrm{py})_{6}(\mathrm{EtOH})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\right]$ $\mathrm{(py~=~}$ pyridine). Because of the very large pores (ca. 28 A˚ —Fig. 10) the material could easily lose solvent molecules when exposed to air. Crystallinity was, however, recovered by immersing the crystals in ethanol or methanol, but not in other organic solvents. The structure is layered, with a honeycomb (6,3) topology, and the intermetallic distances are of ca. 15 (intra-layer) and $9\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ (inter-layer). At ambient temperature the $\chi T$ of MOROF-1 is 1.8 e.m.u. K $\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ indicating that ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and the radical molecules do not interact. Below $250~\mathrm{K}$ the $\chi T$ decreases smoothly up to $31\mathrm{~K~}$ due to ferromagnetic coupling between neighbouring $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ and radicals within the layers. For temperatures lower than $31\mathrm{~K~}$ there is a considerable increase of $\chi T$ indicating a strong long-range magnetic ordering originated by dipolar–dipolar magnetic interactions between neighbouring layers. Such a feature leads to a supramolecular three-dimensional magnetic network which still maintains its two-dimensional character because the observed $\chi T$ strongly depends on the applied external magnetic field.
A year later the same research group produced using $L^{35}$ a second porous compound (MOROF-2) with Co2+ and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine $(4{,}4^{\prime}–\mathrm{bpy})$ as an auxiliary bridging rod: $[\mathrm{Co}(L^{35})(4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{-bpy})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}]\cdot6\mathrm{EtOH}\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ .108 This structure is composed of a one-dimensional polymer in which only one carboxylate of $L^{35}$ coordinates to ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and intermetallic bridges occur via the diamagnetic rods. Such structural features seriously compromise magnetic exchange leading to less exciting (if complicated) magnetic properties: at ambient temperature the $\chi T$ of MOROF-2 is 3.69 e.m.u. $\mathbf{K}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ and, as the temperature is lowered, it decreases smoothly up to 2.68 e.m.u. $\mathrm{\K\mol^{-1}}$ (at $50~\mathrm{K}$ ; below this temperature the decrease is more abrupt reaching 1.22 e.m.u. $\mathbf{K}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ at $1.8\mathrm{K}$ . This behaviour is consistent with a model for a two-spin system (i.e., the radical coordinated to $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ ) with strong antiferromagnetic coupling. To improve the magnetic properties for ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ -based materials the diamagnetic bridge had to be suppressed, and a new material was developed109 in which the $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ centres are instead coordinated to the $L^{35}$ radicals forming a (6,3) helical structure related to the topology of MOROF-1: $[\mathrm{Co}_{6}(L^{35})_{4}(\mathrm{py})_{17}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mathrm{EtOH})]$ (MOROF-3). The $\chi T$ of this material decreases smoothly between ambient temperature and $16\mathrm{K}$ from 22 e.m.u. $\mathbf{K}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ to $13.7\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{K}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , which is consistent with a non-interacting system of six ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and four radicals. For lower temperatures $\chi T$ increases rapidly to 32.7 e.m.u. $\mathbf{K}\ m\mathbf{ol}^{-1}$ (at $1.8\mathrm{~K~},$ . These studies revealed the coexistence of ferro- and antiferromagnetic interactions attributed to the different coordination modes of the radical (bidentate and monodentate).
In short, the bulky trigonal $L^{35}$ ligand can lead to magnetic networks if the magnetic exchange path is not interrupted by diamagnetic ligands. It became clear that in order to expand the connectivity into three-dimensional space it was necessary to devise a radical with more coordination-capable functional groups. The hexa substituted form with the carboxylic groups at the meta positions of the phenyl groups $[L^{36}\mathrm{-tris}(2,4,6-$ trichloro-3,5-dicarboxy)methyl radical—Fig. S31 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dag]^{110}}$ was prepared and self-assembled with ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ : $\mathrm{[Cu_{6}(}L^{36})_{2}(4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}$ b $\mathrm{py})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{12}].$ 111 Surprisingly, because of the high connectivity of the radical, the inclusion of the diamagnetic $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine in this material was not detrimental of the magnetic properties. As in the previous structures, at ambient temperature the $\chi T$ value of 2.94 e.m.u. $\mathrm{~K~mol^{-1}}$ agrees well with a noninteracting set of six ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and two radicals. This feature is maintained up to ca. $50\mathrm{K}$ , then increases to 3.47 e.m.u. $\mathbf{K}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ (at $20~\mathrm{K}$ ). Below this temperature an abrupt increase of $\chi T$ is observed, reaching 7.64 e.m.u. $\mathbf{K}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ at $2\mathrm{~K~}$ , which indicates the presence of metal–radical ferromagnetic exchange interactions. Roques et al. also gave a concise summary of all polymeric structures with radicals available in the literature. The reader is advised to see this reference for additional structural details.111
In summary, a careful selection of radical molecules and appropriate metallic centres may address some of the main concerns in producing large and open hybrid crystalline frameworks without disrupting the magnetic exchange between metallic centres. This organic ligand design approach is now being pursued for different systems such as those with lanthanide centres, with a recent report showing the existence of lanthanide– radical ferromagnetic interactions.112 We envisage that this combination of a radical and a lanthanide could be the key to engineering novel materials with mutually dependent optical and magnetic properties.
## 7 Functional materials based on metal-bearing ligands
This review is focused on the synthesis and design of only traditional organic linkers, i.e., the metallic nodes of MOFs are only interconnected by pure organic bridges. A new, but slowly emergent area of research in the vast field of MOFs focuses on the inclusion of a second type of metallic centre directly coordinated, and ultimately composing, the internodal bridges of the frameworks. It must be stressed that for these linkers to be classified as true metal-bearing ligands the second type of metallic centre should not have any influence on the overall connectivity (and topology) of the framework itself.
An interesting example which illustrates this concept concerns the development of improved catalytic systems based on metal–salen Schiff base complexes, well known as highly active homogeneous catalysts.113 In 2001, Morris et al.114 showed that the formation of supramolecular non-crystalline adducts of $(R,R)$ -(2)-1,2-cyclohexanediamino- $\cdot N,N^{\prime}$ -bis(3-tert-butyl-5- (4-pyridyl)salicylidene) $\mathrm{{Mn}^{\mathrm{{III}}}\mathrm{{Cl}}}$ considerably improved the stability of the chiral catalyst (without compromising the enantioselectivity) and the turnover frequency. Five years later Cho et al.115 developed a synthetic strategy which allowed the immobilization of the same chiral catalyst into a 3D framework using as auxiliary brigand ligand biphenyldicarboxylate $(\mathrm{bpdc}^{2-})$ : $\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(b p d c)_{2}(s a l e n–M n^{I I I})_{\Delta}^{-}}$ $10\mathrm{DMFcdot8H_{2}O}$ . The authors showed that the catalytic activity of this framework was similar to that of the homogeneous catalyst, but the catalysis was a truly heterogeneous process which greatly improved material stability and catalyst recovery and re-use. Other very recent examples within the same range of applications have been reported by Song et al.116 and Huang et al.117 We note that such immobilization of discrete complexes can be expanded to several other applications, as demonstrated by the research groups of Chen and Thomas who designed porous MOFs with highly accessible open metal centres for the adsorption of hydrogen.118 More recently this same group showed that the use of these complexes can lead to framework-type materials with improved selective separation of acetylene and ethylene.119
A more recent example which may find future application in the emerging field of nanomedicine was reported by the research groups of Champness and George:120 the $N,N\mathrm{.}$ -chelated organic ligand $(L)$ of neutral organometallic $\mathrm{Re(I)}$ and $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}(\mathbf{\mathrm{{I}}})$ complexes with CO, $[\mathrm{M}(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Cl}(L)].$ was prior to coordination derivatised at exo (i.e., divergent) positions with carboxylic acid groups, yielding the organic linker $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine- ${\ 5,5^{\prime}}^{\prime}.$ - dicarboxylic acid ( $L^{37}.$ —Fig. S32 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dagger}$ ); this derivatisation permitted a second coordination step to $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ cations (in the presence of DMF as solvent) to form three-dimensional noninterpenetrated MOF architectures retaining the well-known fac-configuration of the central organometallic complex. Because neither $\mathrm{Re(I)}$ nor $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}(\mathbf{\mathrm{I}})$ participate in the construction of the framework, these well-known photoactive complexes can thus be envisaged as being immobilised into a 3D ordered arrangement in the solid state. Such a feature allowed the authors to directly observe in the solid state the fac–mer isomerisation of the complexes after photoinduced release of CO (irradiation with U.V. light).
Iridium complexes are highly phosphorescent because of the metal-to-ligand-charge-transfer process. This can be strongly affected by the presence of certain type of molecules. Having this in mind, Xie et al.121 used 3-(pyridin-2-yl)benzoic acid $(L^{38\mathrm{a}})$ and 4-(pyridin-2-yl)benzoic acid $(L^{38\mathrm{b}})$ to prepare discrete complexes, $[\mathrm{Ir}(L^{38\mathrm{a}})_{3}]$ and $[\mathrm{Ir}(L^{38\mathrm{b}})_{3}]$ (Fig. S33 in the $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ by way of microwave heating. These were then employed as building blocks in the reaction with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ to afford three distinct framework materials: $[\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}\{\mathrm{Ir}(L^{38\mathrm{a}})_{3}\}_{2}]{\cdot}6\mathrm{D}\mathrm{MF}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ $[\mathrm{Zn}_{3}\{\mathrm{Ir}(L^{38\mathrm{b}})_{3}\}_{2}(\mathrm{DEF})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]{\cdot}\mathrm{DEF}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $[Z\mathrm{n}_{3}\{\mathrm{Ir}(L^{38\mathrm{b}})_{3}\}_{2^{-}}$ $(\mathrm{DMF)(H_{2}O)_{3}]\cdot2D M F\cdot3H_{2}O\cdot[I r(\cal L^{38a})_{3}}$ promotes the formation of a highly porous framework which can easily be evacuated and can act as an effective sensor for $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (by quenching the phosphorescence when the material is filled up with this gas). In contrast, even though the structures based on $[\mathrm{Ir}(L^{38\mathrm{b}})_{3}]$ are also porous, the framework apertures are somehow distorted and do not permit a rapid diffusion of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ from the iridium active sites (both in and out). Their potential use as sensors for $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is thus limited. Xie et al.121 clearly showed that a fine tune of the properties of the complexes is pertinent in order to direct the formation of truly porous frameworks with easily accessible active sites so their properties could be exploited in the fabrication of functional devices.
## 8 Conclusion and outlook
The enormous structural diversity of MOFs reported in the literature reflects the high intensity of research in the field. Almost any type of organic molecule with two or more coordinating-capable functional groups may be self-assembled (under suitable synthetic conditions) with metal centres to form polymeric structures. The objective of this review is to examine the broad relationship between the structure of newly designed organic linkers and the properties of the final polymeric structures. The overall conclusions drawn from this literature survey are given below.
The design of novel organic linkers is still greatly focused on the synthesis of highly porous structures, characterized by large intermetallic separations and wide accessible pores. For this, researchers usually rely on symmetrical molecules (often, bi- and tripodal ones). Besides the typically poor thermal and mechanical stability of these highly open frameworks, significant porosity can be detrimental to potential applications: the smaller pores of interpenetrated materials promote more energetically favourable MOF–guest interactions, such as those involved in the adsorption of hydrogen. Even though asymmetry of the organic PBU can still lead to porous and highly symmetrical structures, while avoiding interpenetration, we found that the design of unsymmetric organic linkers is not fully explored with respect not only to geometry (e.g., the location of the functional groups) but also to the type and nature of the coordination-capable moieties.
For the adsorption and separation of gases, the design of organic linkers with polarizable sections (typically those which will line the inner surfaces of the MOFs) can potentiate the observed properties. Some striking examples: ${\bf N}{=}{\bf N}$ bonds (instead of $\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{C}$ ) can act as anchoring sites for hydrogen, with the same being observed for imidazolate-based frameworks; the presence of free and uncoordinated nitrogen atoms may also promote the sequestration of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . The inclusion and separation of gases relies strongly on the MOF–guest interactions because the guest sizes vary little. Separation can thus be achieved in an effective way by the existence of unsaturated metal centres obtained by the thermal activation of the parent materials. It was also shown that biologically relevant gases such as $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and NO interact more strongly with electron-rich MOF surfaces.
Photoluminescence and magnetism are properties which depend strongly on both the organic linkers and the metallic centres. Magnetic exchange between metallic centres in highly porous structures can only be achieved if the centres communicate via the organic backbone. We have shown that this can be achieved by the use of organic radicals which do not disrupt the magnetic exchange, by contrast with the more commonly employed diamagnetic organic linkers. The design of organic PBUs to improve the photoluminescent properties of lanthanide-based frameworks by the antenna effect is also largely unexplored. Most studies of the optical properties of lanthanide-based MOFs are superficial and do not establish important parameters such as quantum yields and lifetime measurements. Conversely, the preparation of frameworks with luminescence based on the organic linkers is a growing area of research. In this context a handful of interesting materials have been prepared by careful selection and design of the molecular cores of the organic linkers. As a result, the photoluminescent properties of the products were highly sensitive to the adsorbed species. These results open the possibility of using MOFs as sensors for organic species. Remarkably, there are no studies on compounds which simultaneously combine magnetic and photoluminescent properties. We envisage that the radical molecules described in this review, which do not possess $\mathrm{C-H}$ quenchers, will stimulate the study of photoluminescent and magnetic properties.
The use of MOFs as heterogeneous catalysts or as zeotype materials depends largely on their ability to be evacuated and maintain structural integrity and on the presence of suitable anchoring intra-channel active sites for catalysis or ion exchange. The latter functionality is strongly dependent on the charge of the organic linker. For example, if the level of deprotonation is high, the networks are likely to be negatively charged and, as observed for zeolites, cations have to be included in the channels to neutralise the crystals. Hence, organic linkers with a large number of –OH groups, such as phosphonates, are typically employed in the design of such compounds. Although many interesting zeolitic MOFs have been described, the properties of the frameworks themselves were not sufficiently explored. For example, cation exchange could be monitored by studying the variations in the photoluminescent properties of lanthanide-based zeolitic MOFs.
The design of organic linkers for the development of heterogeneous catalysts is also very important. Striking examples concern the use of chiral organic PBUs which induce the formation of frameworks capable of catalyzing enantioselective trans-esterification, promote ring opening in meso-carboxylic anhydrides or catalyze the cyanosilylation of aldehydes. Phosphonatebased materials are highly active in Friedel–Crafts alkylations, and
Kitagawa and collaborators showed that highly catalytically active materials can be obtained through careful control of the moieties lining the inner channels of MOFs. This latter work addressed directly the interactions with the guest species and, consequently, the stabilisation of the intermediate molecules of the catalytic cycle. We believe that for effective design of highly active MOF catalysts the organic PBUs must take into account the possible interactions with the known intermediate species of a given transformation.
Discrete complexes have been studied and investigated for several applications for many decades, and several interesting families have been reported. The design of functional MOF architectures by directly transposing these complexes into the chemistry of the building blocks is an emergent field of research. The various examples in the literature indicate that this strategy allows better control of the properties of the final network, since the building blocks (i.e., derivatised complexes) are rigid, chemically stable and maintain their activity in the MOF structure. We envisage that this strategy will be developed in the years to come because it takes real advantage of the vast library of existing knowledge on functional discrete complexes.
Overall, a major obstacle in the design of new organic PBUs seems to reside in the fact that the large quantities of active linkers required for the optimisation of the synthesis of a new material are usually difficult to obtain. Another problem concerns the scaling-up of the routine laboratory-scale synthesis procedures of interesting MOFs for the economically viable industrial use. Even though a handful of compounds can now be produced on a large scale,122–124 and microwave heating is now arising as a promising heating alternative,125 the case of each individual system needs to be studied. Just as crystal engineers collaborated in the investigation of the properties of new materials, we believe that the future will see closer links between organic and inorganic chemists, and engineers anxious to design and prepare new molecules to improve the performance of functional MOFs.
